#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102031 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b27482c3-602c-5761-9cd5-bec55b2c2932" :tutorial/math-academy-id 947 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
